<!--
    kuna-wrapsplit: accesses that run past the top of their address space.

    Issue #1034: decompile-all of a large MSVC DLL panicked in
    Heritage::build_refinement ("index out of bounds: the len is 7 but the
    index is 7") and failed other functions with "Overlapping input varnodes"
    (one naming ram[0xfffffffffffffffb,+0x8)).

    Stage / sub-stage: P3 heritage, the disjoint cover of a heritaged space
    (strict bug fix, no option).

    Address arithmetic wraps, so rsp-2 with 8 bytes is stack bytes -2..-1 and
    0..5, and the qword at 0xfffffffffffffffb is ram bytes 0xff..fb..0xff..ff
    and 0..2. Heritage's range maps are linear: the wrapping range never merged
    with the ranges at offset 0, so the cover overlapped itself.

    The .text bytes below, assembled with nasm (BITS 64):

        f:  mov rax,[rsp-2]             ; stack -2..+6, wraps
            movzx ecx,word [rsp-2]
            mov edx,[rsp+3]
            add rax,rcx
            add rax,rdx
            ret
        g:  mov eax,[rsp+1]
            mov ecx,[rsp-4]
            movups xmm0,[rsp-0xc]       ; its high half wraps
            movups [rsp-0x30],xmm0
            add eax,ecx
            add eax,[rsp-0x30]
            ret
        h:  mov ecx,[qword 0]
            mov rax,[qword 0xfffffffffffffffb]
            add eax,ecx
            ret
        k:  movups [rsp-0xc],xmm0       ; writes stack -0xc..+4
            mov rax,[rsp]
            add eax,[rsp-8]
            ret

    #1 f: the refinement of the wrapping range cut a 6-byte partition at
    offset 0 that also collected the dword at rsp+3; refining it indexed past
    its array (the panic). The read is now the 6 bytes from offset 0 over the
    2 below it, and the bytes from rsp+3 are the dword's low 3 bytes.
    #2 g: renaming asked for an input at [0,4) over the input at [1,5) and
    failed with "Overlapping input varnodes".
    #3 h: the same failure in ram ("requested range ... overflows").
    #4 k: no error, but the return read stack+0 as an uninitialized value: the
    write across the top never reached it. It is now bytes 12..15 of xmm0.
-->
